WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:05.852
Foxconn, best known as the maker of Apple's
iPhone and iPad. They're the biggest in the world.

00:00:05.852 --> 00:00:11.668
They're coming into Wisconsin with an unbelievable plant like we've never seen before.

00:00:11.668 --> 00:00:18.220
In 2018, Donald Trump teamed up with then Wisconsin Governor Scott Walker to offer 4.5 billion dollars

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:22.811
in subsidies and tax incentives to the Taiwanese manufacturer, Foxconn,

00:00:22.811 --> 00:00:28.480
in exchange for building a 20 million square foot factory in the rural community of Mount Pleasant.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:31.435
We can say this is the eighth wonder of the
world.

00:00:31.435 --> 00:00:35.141
The project was supposed to employ 13,000 local workers,

00:00:35.141 --> 00:00:40.785
helping to make good on Trump's campaign promise to increase the number of domestic factory jobs.

00:00:40.785 --> 00:00:44.315
More than two years later, planners have flattened a residential neighborhood

00:00:44.315 --> 00:00:50.646
and built an unfinished data center and warehouses a fraction the size originally planned.

00:00:50.646 --> 00:00:56.435
Foxconn's promise in 2018 was to build a state-of-the-art LCD factory.

00:00:56.435 --> 00:01:01.382
Then it scaled back that plan to a mostly automated smart manufacturing plant.

00:01:01.382 --> 00:01:06.065
After Foxconn failed to reach employment benchmarks, Democratic Governor Tony Evers,

00:01:06.065 --> 00:01:12.177
who defeated Scott Walker in 2018, clawed back some of the subsidies that his predecessor had promised,

00:01:12.177 --> 00:01:18.157
and the project morphed into this. A warehouse one twentieth the size of what was originally promised,

00:01:18.157 --> 00:01:24.839
which is being used not for manufacturing, but for storage, according to an investigative article in The Verge.

00:01:24.839 --> 00:01:30.724
This debacle is just the latest government-led development deal riddled with false promises

00:01:30.724 --> 00:01:34.908
in which the state provided rich giveaways and used the threat of eminent domain

00:01:34.908 --> 00:01:38.637
to push citizens out of their homes to benefit a private company.

00:01:38.637 --> 00:01:46.024
The Mount Pleasant neighborhood, where the Foxconn factory was supposed to be built was flattened, so far for no reason.

00:01:46.024 --> 00:01:49.032
When Reason did a story on the project in 2018,

00:01:49.032 --> 00:01:54.334
Kim and Jim Mahoney were one of the only families left within one and a half square miles. 

00:01:54.334 --> 00:01:55.775
They were fighting a local government

00:01:55.775 --> 00:02:02.339
attempting to bully them to sell their dream home on the grounds the state could also just step in and force them out. 

00:02:02.339 --> 00:02:07.343
How can they take my house? To me, it's stealing.

00:02:07.343 --> 00:02:12.191
We just built this home. We're at the top of our budget. It just doesn't seem fair.

00:02:12.191 --> 00:02:18.941
The Mahoney's, unlike all their neighbors, managed to stay put, and today their house overlooks an unfinished construction site.

00:02:18.941 --> 00:02:22.337
It's unclear what ultimately will become of the project.

00:02:22.337 --> 00:02:27.232
What you're doing is you're setting people up for failure if you encourage them to locate in a place

00:02:27.232 --> 00:02:30.550
where they fundamentally shouldn't be locating.

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:34.455
Economist, Matthew Mitchell, from the Mercatus Center at George Mason University,

00:02:34.455 --> 00:02:36.916
coauthored a study on the Foxconn deal.

00:02:36.916 --> 00:02:42.325
He says, "It's a cautionary tale of what happens when politicians become private sector deal makers.

00:02:42.325 --> 00:02:47.756
You're not spending your own money and you don't reap any upside reward from the benefits.

00:02:47.756 --> 00:02:53.494
So you have no incentive to minimize the costs and no incentive to maximize the benefits.

00:02:53.494 --> 00:02:58.885
"That which yields political profit," Mitchell says, "is considered a cost in business."

00:02:58.885 --> 00:03:02.439
If a private entrepreneur went to you and said, "I've got this great plan.

00:03:02.439 --> 00:03:07.235
I would like you to invest 3.6 billion dollars into my company, and here's the best part,

00:03:07.235 --> 00:03:10.193
it would employ 13,000 people. Isn't that awesome?"

00:03:10.193 --> 00:03:12.874
You would say, "Why are you talking to me about the costs?"

00:03:12.874 --> 00:03:16.972
What the public never sees at a ribbon cutting ceremony is all the economic activity

00:03:16.972 --> 00:03:23.119
that will never take place because of the burden that taxpayer subsidies shift onto other businesses and activities.

00:03:23.119 --> 00:03:28.763
Mitchell estimates that the Foxconn deal contained $2.8 billion in outright subsidies.

00:03:28.763 --> 00:03:36.296
The taxation needed to fund them would have reduced Wisconsin's GDP by about $20 billion over that same period.

00:03:36.296 --> 00:03:40.952
You're removing money from the pockets of consumers and other taxpayers,

00:03:40.952 --> 00:03:44.861
who they themselves would go out and create multiplier activities.

00:03:44.861 --> 00:03:49.881
But using government funds to broker business deals remains popular among politicians.

00:03:49.881 --> 00:03:56.553
One survey of American mayors found that 84% favored using the government for targeted economic development.

00:03:56.553 --> 00:03:58.761
The very best thing that a governor could do

00:03:58.761 --> 00:04:05.751
is to announce that they will never, ever preside over any ribbon cutting ceremony in which they subsidize a particular firm.

00:04:05.751 --> 00:04:09.348
Most governors don't appreciate that, and most taxpayers don't appreciate that.

00:04:09.348 --> 00:04:15.132
While that may make the most sense economically, I fully admit it does not necessarily make sense politically.

00:04:15.132 --> 00:04:20.670
There is a way to get around this, that sort of appreciates the political economy of the situation.

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:27.326
States could enter into interstate compacts with one another, whereby they agree to a mutually disarm in the subsidy war.

00:04:27.326 --> 00:04:30.103
I'll put away my subsidies, if you put away yours.

00:04:30.103 --> 00:04:35.491
This is really the same kind of logic that governs things like the WTO and it's been extraordinarily successful.

00:04:35.491 --> 00:04:40.721
Mitchell says that the Foxconn debacle isn't a story about Trump's unique incompetence.

00:04:40.721 --> 00:04:47.422
It's about the perils of government-led business deals, no matter who's in charge and what political party they belong to.

00:04:47.422 --> 00:04:52.583
Democratic politicians in the Connecticut town of New London partnered with a Republican governor

00:04:52.583 --> 00:04:59.553
to seize the land of homeowners like Suzette Kelo, in order to then hand it over to the pharmaceutical giant, Pfizer.

00:04:59.553 --> 00:05:06.088
The Pfizer project was never built, and this is how the lot where Kelo's house once stood looks 13 years later.

00:05:06.088 --> 00:05:12.433
In 1981, Detroit and Hamtramck politicians
partnered with General Motors to used eminent domain 

00:05:12.433 --> 00:05:17.830
to displace more than 4,000 people in the predominantly immigrant neighborhood of Poletown

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:20.217
to make way for a car assembly plant.

00:05:20.217 --> 00:05:28.455
And in 2009, the Obama administration directed a 535 million government loan to the solar manufacturer Solyndra

00:05:28.455 --> 00:05:33.066
to construct a state-of-the-art manufacturing facility in California,

00:05:33.066 --> 00:05:39.434
but the company went bankrupt soon after, costing US taxpayers more than half a billion dollars.

00:05:39.434 --> 00:05:44.914
Democrats were very enthusiastic about the subsidies to Solyndra during the Obama years,

00:05:44.914 --> 00:05:50.119
and they were very down on the subsidies to Carrier and Foxconn during the Trump years.

00:05:50.119 --> 00:05:57.493
Unfortunately, I think that partisans see target economic development incentives pretty clearly

00:05:57.493 --> 00:06:02.530
when the other side is doing it, but they have an extraordinary blind spot when it comes to their own incentives.

